{"causes": ["The Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth's attempts to limit the number and autonomy of Registered Cossacks fueled widespread discontent.", "Religious tensions between the predominantly Orthodox Cossacks and the Catholic-dominated Polish nobility exacerbated social and political grievances.", "The construction of the Kodak Fortress in 1635 symbolized an overt and unwelcome Polish attempt to control the strategic Zaporozhian Sich."], "effects": ["Sulyma's execution led to increased Polish repression against the Cossacks, including the strengthening and re-garrisoning of Kodak Fortress.", "His uprising and martyrdom solidified Ivan Sulyma's status as a powerful symbol of Cossack defiance and a fight for self-determination.", "The events of 1635 contributed significantly to the growing unrest and resentment that culminated in the massive Khmelnytsky Uprising of 1648."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "zaporozhian-cossacks", "sourceName": "Zaporozhian Cossacks", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "ivan-sulyma", "targetName": "Ivan Sulyma", "context": "Ivan Sulyma's leadership and worldview were profoundly shaped by the martial traditions and autonomous spirit of the Zaporozhian Cossacks."}, {"sourceSlug": "ivan-sulyma", "sourceName": "Ivan Sulyma", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "sulyma-uprising", "targetName": "Sulyma Uprising", "context": "Ivan Sulyma was the principal leader of the 1635 Sulyma Uprising against the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth."}, {"sourceSlug": "ivan-sulyma", "sourceName": "Ivan Sulyma", "verb": "TRANSFORMS", "targetSlug": "kodak-fortress", "targetName": "Kodak Fortress", "context": "Ivan Sulyma led the surprise attack and destruction of the strategic Kodak Fortress in August 1635."}, {"sourceSlug": "polish-lithuanian-commonwealth", "sourceName": "Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "ivan-sulyma", "targetName": "Ivan Sulyma", "context": "The repressive policies and attempts to control Cossack autonomy by the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth directly provoked Ivan Sulyma's rebellion."}, {"sourceSlug": "ivan-sulyma", "sourceName": "Ivan Sulyma", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "ukraine", "targetName": "Ukraine", "context": "Ivan Sulyma's entire historical impact and rebellion unfolded within the geographical and political context of Ukraine, then part of the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth."}], "places": [{"name": "Zaporizhzhia, Ukraine", "role": "Cossack base of operations"}, {"name": "Kodak, Ukraine", "role": "Site of fortress capture"}, {"name": "Warsaw, Poland", "role": "Place of execution"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Ivan Sulyma", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "ivan-sulyma"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q2066175", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ivan_Sulyma"], "tags": ["Political Leaders", "political-systems", "geopolitical-linkage", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Ivan Sulyma's daring 1635 uprising and subsequent martyrdom significantly escalated tensions between the Zaporozhian Cossacks and the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth, directly contributing to the preconditions for the Khmelnytsky Uprising and the eventual loss of Polish control over large parts of Ukraine.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-09-06T06:26:11Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#34016356939", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Ivan Sulyma was a Senior of Registered Cossacks in 1628–29 and a Kosh Otaman in 1630–1635.", "newValue": "Ivan Sulyma, a pivotal figure in early 17th-century Ukrainian history, served as a Senior of Registered Cossacks in 1628–29 and later as a Kosh Otaman of the Zaporozhian Host from 1630 to 1635. His significance stems from his staunch defiance of Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth authority and his leadership of the 1635 Sulyma Uprising, a dramatic assertion of Cossack autonomy against increasing royal control. Sulyma's actions, particularly the audacious capture and destruction of the Kodak Fortress, positioned him as a symbol of Cossack resistance and a precursor to the larger struggles for Ukrainian independence.

Sulyma's most defining act occurred in August 1635 when, returning from service against the Ottoman Empire, he orchestrated a surprise attack on the newly constructed Kodak Fortress, a key Polish stronghold designed to control the Zaporozhian Sich. The fortress, intended to prevent Cossack raids and maintain order, was razed, and its 200-man garrison, including French engineer Guillaume Le Vasseur de Beauplan, was massacred. This act of overt rebellion shocked the Commonwealth, leading to a swift and brutal response. Hetman Stanisław Koniecpolski's forces quickly suppressed the uprising, and Sulyma, betrayed by some of his own Cossacks, was captured and transported to Warsaw.

His public execution in Warsaw on December 12, 1635, where he was quartered, solidified his image as a martyr for Cossack liberty and Orthodox faith. Sulyma's uprising, though short-lived and ultimately unsuccessful, underscored the deep-seated grievances among the Zaporozhian Cossacks regarding their limited autonomy, religious persecution, and social status under Polish rule. It served as a potent, if tragic, example of Cossack resolve, contributing to the escalating tensions that would erupt into the much larger Khmelnytsky Uprising a decade later."}, {"timestamp": "2026-09-06T06:26:11Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#34016356939", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Early Modern era: age of exploration, printing revolution, and religious reformation", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1600, Rogoshchi"}, {"title": "Political crises and power transitions requiring new leadership", "type": "Idea", "year": "1600, Rogoshchi"}], "newValue": ["The Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth's attempts to limit the number and autonomy of Registered Cossacks fueled widespread discontent.", "Religious tensions between the predominantly Orthodox Cossacks and the Catholic-dominated Polish nobility exacerbated social and political grievances.", "The construction of the Kodak Fortress in 1635 symbolized an overt and unwelcome Polish attempt to control the strategic Zaporozhian Sich."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-09-06T06:26:11Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#34016356939", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Shaped political institutions and governance practices", "type": "Idea", "year": "1635-12-12"}, {"title": "Contributed to global exchange networks, modern state formation, and scientific revolution", "type": "Movement", "year": "1635-12-12"}], "newValue": ["Sulyma's execution led to increased Polish repression against the Cossacks, including the strengthening and re-garrisoning of Kodak Fortress.", "His uprising and martyrdom solidified